---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';

const path = '/guides';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'guides');
const desc = description(site, 'guides');

const guides = [
  {
    "slug": "tcpa",
    "title": "TCPA Compliance for Shopify SMS",
    "kicker": "TCPA & legal risk"
  },
  {
    "slug": "quiet-hours",
    "title": "Quiet Hours & Timezone SMS Sending",
    "kicker": "Quiet hours"
  },
  {
    "slug": "consent",
    "title": "SMS Consent Collection on Shopify",
    "kicker": "Consent architecture"
  },
  {
    "slug": "pricing",
    "title": "SMS Pricing & Cost-Per-Message Models",
    "kicker": "Message economics"
  },
  {
    "slug": "list-growth",
    "title": "SMS List Growth Without Consent Debt",
    "kicker": "List growth"
  },
  {
    "slug": "two-way-sms",
    "title": "Two-Way SMS & Reply Handling",
    "kicker": "Two-way SMS"
  },
  {
    "slug": "email-sms-coordination",
    "title": "Email + SMS Coordination Playbook",
    "kicker": "Channel coordination"
  },
  {
    "slug": "deliverability",
    "title": "SMS Deliverability & Carrier Filtering",
    "kicker": "Deliverability"
  },
  {
    "slug": "discount-discipline",
    "title": "SMS Discount Discipline",
    "kicker": "Discount discipline"
  },
  {
    "slug": "segmentation",
    "title": "SMS Segmentation on Shopify",
    "kicker": "Segmentation"
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="py-16 md:py-24">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="badge">Operator playbooks</p>
      <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Shopify SMS guides — TCPA, consent, pricing, coordination</h1>
      <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl text-cyan-100">Find the guide for the decision blocking your program. Use these planning aids to prepare a provider demonstration and specialist review, then keep unresolved launch requirements visible.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-16 grid gap-4 md:grid-cols-2">
    {guides.map(g => (
      <a class="card" href={`/guides/${g.slug}`}>
        <p class="badge">{g.kicker}</p>
        <h2 class="mt-3 text-xl font-black text-white">{g.title}</h2>
      </a>
    ))}
  </section>

  <article class="container prose-shopify card mb-16">
    <h2>Start with the unresolved decision</h2>
    <p>A store choosing software needs a different reading path from an operator investigating a failed flow. The cards above cover individual topics; use the paths below to connect the outputs without treating any guide as approval to send.</p>
    <h3>Planning a new program</h3>
    <p>Start with <a href="/guides/consent">consent collection</a> and <a href="/guides/tcpa">legal review planning</a>. Prepare the proposed audience, collection surfaces, message purpose and markets for the responsible reviewer. Then use <a href="/guides/quiet-hours">quiet hours</a> to list timing and location questions for the provider. These pages do not establish current legal requirements for your particular program.</p>
    <h3>Choosing or replacing an app</h3>
    <p>Use <a href="/guides/pricing">pricing</a> to make comparable normal-month and peak-month quotes. Bring your event names, destination mix, message creative and reply requirements to the demonstration. Open the <a href="/apps">app catalog</a> after writing that brief; a starting price or a feature badge cannot resolve whether your actual data reaches the flow.</p>
    <h3>Repairing an existing workflow</h3>
    <p>For messages arriving after a purchase or support conversation, begin with <a href="/guides/email-sms-coordination">channel coordination</a> and <a href="/guides/segmentation">segmentation</a>. For unanswered replies, use <a href="/guides/two-way-sms">reply handling</a>. For missing messages, use <a href="/guides/deliverability">delivery investigation</a> to organize the provider conversation rather than assuming a new sender fixes the cause.</p>
    <h3>Growing without confusing revenue and profit</h3>
    <p>Read <a href="/guides/list-growth">list growth</a> alongside <a href="/guides/discount-discipline">discount discipline</a>. Separate acquisition source, offer redemption, fulfilled orders and opt-outs. A larger audience and more attributed revenue are inputs to evaluation, not evidence that the additional messages created profitable orders.</p>
    <h2>Leave each guide with an artifact</h2>
    <p>Keep a single decision log: the unresolved question, the evidence needed, its owner and what changes if the answer is unfavorable. Attach a quote for a billing question, an event trace for a stale-state question, and the responsible reviewer's decision for a legal question. Record unknowns instead of turning a suggested workflow into a claimed integration.</p>
    <p>For a proposed demonstration, queue an internal cart reminder, complete the purchase and open a service case before its send time. Ask the provider to show the cancellation decision and reply ownership across channels. This is an evaluation idea, not a test we ran or a universal launch checklist. Category-specific constraints belong in the <a href="/for">store-type playbooks</a>.</p>
    <p>Some guides remain under editorial review. Use current provider documentation and responsible specialist advice for the actual program; links here do not certify a sibling page's accuracy or readiness to publish.</p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
